2️⃣ Backend Config (API with upstream)
upstream backend_app {
    server 127.0.0.1:5000;
    # server 127.0.0.1:5001; # optional second backend
    keepalive 16;
}


upstream → Defines backend server(s) for load balancing.

keepalive 16 → Reuse 16 connections for performance.

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name api.example.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}


HTTP → HTTPS redirect for backend.

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name api.example.com;


HTTPS server for backend API.

HTTP/2 enabled.

    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/api.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/api.example.com/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers 'ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256:ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256';
    ssl_prefer_server_ciphers on;
    ssl_session_cache shared:SSL:10m;


SSL settings same as frontend (secure TLS, strong ciphers, session cache).

    access_log /var/log/nginx/backend_access.log;
    error_log /var/log/nginx/backend_error.log;


Separate logs for backend API.

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend_app;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_cache_bypass $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_connect_timeout 10s;
        proxy_send_timeout 30s;
        proxy_read_timeout 30s;
        client_max_body_size 50M;
    }


proxy_pass http://backend_app; → Forward requests to upstream backend.

proxy_set_header → Pass client info and headers to backend.

timeouts → Handle slow clients safely.

client_max_body_size → Allow large uploads.

    location /health {
        proxy_pass http://backend_app/health;
    }


Optional health check endpoint for monitoring backend status.

    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN" always;
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
    add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block" always;
    add_header Referrer-Policy "no-referrer-when-downgrade" always;
    add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;


  Let’s break them line by line:

1️⃣ add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN" always;

Purpose: Prevents your website from being embedded in <iframe>s on other domains.

Why: Stops clickjacking attacks.

SAMEORIGIN → Only allow embedding your own pages, not from other sites.

always → Ensures header is sent even on errors.

2️⃣ add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;

Purpose: Stops browsers from trying to “guess” the content type of a file.

Why: Prevents MIME-type sniffing attacks (e.g., executing malicious scripts).

nosniff → Tells browser to trust the declared Content-Type header.

3️⃣ add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block" always;

Purpose: Enables the browser’s built-in XSS (cross-site scripting) filter.

1 → Enable XSS filter.

mode=block → If an attack is detected, block rendering the page.

Note: Modern browsers have mostly deprecated this because they rely on Content-Security-Policy instead, but it’s still harmless to include.

4️⃣ add_header Referrer-Policy "no-referrer-when-downgrade" always;

Purpose: Controls what referrer information (the previous page URL) is sent when users click links.

no-referrer-when-downgrade → Send the referrer only when navigating from HTTPS → HTTPS. Don’t send it when going from HTTPS → HTTP.

Why: Protects privacy and avoids leaking sensitive URLs.

5️⃣ add_header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload" always;

Purpose: Enforces HTTPS.

max-age=31536000 → Browsers will remember to use HTTPS for 1 year.

includeSubDomains → Applies HTTPS enforcement to all subdomains.

preload → Allows your domain to be added to browser preload lists (HTTPS-only by default).
